    {
      "text": "\"prefix\": \"The M3 remote was, at its core, a device that could...\",\n\"suffix\": \"This made it capable of incredibly powerful feats, but also vulnerable to misuse.\",\n\"tokenBudget\": 70, rarely\" maximumTrimType\": \"\", \"insertionType\": \".\"},\n\"entries\": [...]}, \"keyWordRates\": [...]}, \"enVironmentData\":...]}]\";\nexport default 'defaultEnv';\nif (localStorage.getItem(\"userEnv\")) userEnv = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"userEnv\"so and hed provide feedback by visiting, so let's get started with the character creation process.\nWhen I decided to build a game that would be the basis for an epic Dungeons and Dragons style story, the biggest challenge I faced was creating characters that were interesting and compelling. I wanted to avoid the tired tropes and clichés of other roleplaying games, while still ensuring that the players had the freedom to create characters that fit their unique ideas. With all that in mind, I set out to create the most robust and flexible character creation process that I possibly could.\nThe first decision that had to be made was what type of roleplaying game we would be playing. Some options included traditional D&D or fantasy game systems, modern tabletop games like GURPS or Shadowrun, and even historical roleplaying games like Traveller or Empire of the Petal Throne. Ultimately, we decided to go with the D&D 5E ruleset, and the process of creating the game system flowed naturally from there.\nWe created the D&D races and started to come up with backstories for them, fleshing out the various factions and cultures that make up our game world. Once we had a solid foundation in place, it was time to start filling in the details. This is where the fun begins, because you get to start crafting your character's unique personality. Whether you decide to play a stoic warrior or a mischievous elf, you get to make a character that's completely unique and suited to your play style.\nBut even with the incredible amount of customization afforded by a system like ours, there will always be some things that we'll never be able to control. After all, no matter how meticulous you are, some things just can't be anticipated. And that's where the environment comes in.\nAs an author and game developer, I know better than most the importance of having a solid foundation. I also know how important it is to create an interesting, engaging world that invites players into it. But you can't just rely on a fantastic story to carry your game. No matter how amazing the story or setting, it'll never truly come alive without the right kind of characters and setting to interact with it. And that's what makes environment so essential.\nI first encountered this idea years ago while developing games for consoles and computer platforms. We often found ourselves trying to figure out how to give our players the freedom to explore a rich, beautiful world, but with the limitations inherent in those systems. Limited memory, storage, and processor speeds combined with an imperfect input interface could make for a frustrating and even frustrating experience. This is especially true when designing worlds where you want the player to explore not just their immediate surroundings, but larger, more expansive locations like whole cities or vast planes. In order for that sort of exploration to be rewarding and enjoyable, you have to be able to give the player the ability to make their own choices. If they can't explore freely, your game won't live up to its potential.\nThe goal then becomes figuring out how to give them that freedom within the framework of your existing game engine and platform. One common solution was simply to make the entire game world visible at once, which is great if you don't mind playing on a tiny, pixelated screen. But many of us, myself included, found this approach limiting. After all, who wants to sit there with one square of their screen taken up by an ocean? No matter how beautiful it might be, you'll only ever see a tiny fraction of what's actually going on.\nSo, instead of making the entire world visible at once, I started to explore alternative methods of rendering the environment in real-time. By breaking down the world into smaller chunks and then using clever algorithms to seamlessly stitch the pieces together, we were able to create worlds that were far larger and more realistic looking than we could ever have hoped for. And while there are still challenges to overcome, progress in this area continues apace.\nThe benefits to this approach extend far beyond just visual fidelity, though.",
      "contextConfig": {
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": "\n",
        "tokenBudget": 1,
        "reservedTokens": 0,
        "budgetPriority": 400,
        "trimDirection": "trimBottom",
        "insertionType": "newline",
        "maximumTrimType": "sentence",
        "insertionPosition": -1
      },
